// The control client. Outside tools steer the running shell through it.
export anchor Control:
    purpose:
        Let a compositor key binding, or a script, tell the running shell what to do.
        Wayland apps cannot grab global keys, so this is how the launcher is opened.

    how:
        The shell binary also works as a command line client.
        The client sends the request over a Unix socket to the running shell.
        The binary is named osmia.
        A command is written as osmia, then the part, then the action.
        Examples are osmia launcher toggle, osmia dnd on, and osmia history toggle.

    socket:
        The socket is in the user runtime folder, for example XDG_RUNTIME_DIR.
        Only the owner of the session can use it.
        There is no way to open it to other users.

    status:
        The client can also ask the shell for its state.
        An example is osmia dnd status, which says whether do-not-disturb is on.
        The answer is plain text, or JSON when the user asks for it.
        There is no live event stream in the first version.

    commands:
        - Toggle the launcher
        - Toggle the notification history
        - Turn do-not-disturb on, off, or toggle it
        - Reload the config
        - Ask for the state of a part

    failure:
        If no shell is running, the client prints an error and exits with a failure code.

    openQuestions:
        - Confirm the commands, exit codes, and JSON status in ProposedDefaults.
        - Short forms for commands.
        - Whether a live event stream is added later.
